There are some irregular nouns that change their vowel from short/lax in the singular to long/tense in the plural. Their spelling does not alternate between single and double letters. However, the sound {{IPA|/ɪ/}} becomes {{IPA|/eː/}} in the plural of such nouns instead of {{IPA|/iː/}}, which is reflected in the spelling.
* {{lang|nl|dag}} {{IPA|/dɑx/}} ("day"), ''da-gen'' {{IPA|/ˈdaː.ɣə(n)/}} ("days")
* {{lang|nl|stad}} {{IPA|/stɑt/}} ("city, town"), ''ste-den'' {{IPA|/steːdə(n)/}} ("cities, towns")
* {{lang|nl|weg}} {{IPA|/ʋɛx/}} ("road, way"), ''we-gen'' {{IPA|/ˈʋeː.ɣə(n)/}} ("roads, ways")
* {{lang|nl|schip}} {{IPA|/sxɪp/}} ("ship"), ''sche-pen'' {{IPA|/ˈsxeː.pə(n)/}} ("ships")
* {{lang|nl|lot}} {{IPA|/lɔt/}} ("lottery ticket"), ''lo-ten'' {{IPA|/ˈloː.tə(n)/}} ("lottery tickets")

===Exceptions===
As a rule, the simplest representation is always chosen. A double vowel is never written in an open syllable, and a double consonant is never written at the end of a word or when next to another consonant. A double vowel is rarely followed by a double consonant, as it could be simplified by writing them both single.

The past tense of verbs may have a double vowel, followed by a double consonant, to distinguish those forms from the present tense.
* ''ha-ten'' ("hate"), ''haat-ten'' ("hated"), both {{IPA|/ˈɦaː.tə(n)/}}
* ''ra-den'' ("guess"), ''raad-den'' ("guessed"), both {{IPA|/ˈraː.də(n)/}}

Compounds should be read as if each word were spelled separately, and they may therefore appear to violate the normal rules. That may sometimes cause confusion if the word is not known to be a compound.
* ''dag-ar-bei-der'' {{IPA|/ˈdɑx.ˌɑr.bɛi.dər/}} or more fluently {{IPA|/ˈdɑ.ˌɣɑr.bɛi.dər/}} ("day labourer"), a compound of {{lang|nl|dag}} ("day") + {{lang|nl|arbeider}} ("labourer") so it is not divided as ''*da-gar-bei-der'' {{IPA|*/ˈdaː.ˌɣɑr.bɛi.dər/}}. If it were not a compound, it would be written ''*dag-gar-bei-der'' to keep the first "a" short.
* ''een-en-twin-tig'' {{IPA|/ˈeː.nən.ˌtʋɪn.təx/}} ("twenty-one"), a compound of {{lang|nl|een}} ("one") + {{lang|nl|en}} ("and") + {{lang|nl|twintig}} ("twenty"). If it were not a compound, it would be written ''*e-nen-twin-tig'' to avoid having a double vowel at the end of a syllable.
* ''mee-doen'' {{IPA|/ˈmeː.dun/}} ("to participate"), a compound of {{lang|nl|mee}} ("along (with)") + {{lang|nl|doen}} ("do"). If it were not a compound, it would be written ''*me-doen'' to avoid having a double vowel at the end of a syllable. The word {{lang|nl|mee}} itself has a double vowel because of the exception with final -{{vr|e}}, as noted above.

==Final devoicing and the'' 't&nbsp;kofschip'' rule==
{{main|Final obstruent devoicing|'t kofschip}}
{{Unreferenced section|date=March 2020}}
Final devoicing is not indicated in Dutch spelling; words are usually spelled according to the historically original consonant. Therefore, a word may be written with a letter for a voiced consonant at the end of a word but still be pronounced with a voiceless consonant:

* {{lang|nl|heb}} {{IPA|/ɦɛp/}} "(I) have" but {{lang|nl|hebben}} {{IPA|/ˈɦɛbə(n)/}} "to have"
* {{lang|nl|paard}} {{IPA|/paːrt/}} "horse" but {{lang|nl|paarden}} {{IPA|/ˈpaːrdə(n)/}} "horses"
* {{lang|nl|leg}} {{IPA|/lɛx/}} "(I) lay" but {{lang|nl|leggen}} {{IPA|/ˈlɛɣə(n)/}} "to lay"

Weak verbs form their past tense and past participle by addition of a dental, {{vr|d}} or {{vr|t}} depending on the [[voice (phonetics)|voicing]] of the preceding consonant(s) (see [[Assimilation (linguistics)|assimilation]]). However, because final consonants are always devoiced, there is no difference in pronunciation between these in the participle. Nonetheless, in accordance with the above rules, the orthography operates as if the consonant were still voiced. The same dental consonant letter is spelled in the past participle as in the past tense forms in which it is not word-final. To help memorise when to write {{vr|d}} and when {{vr|t}}, Dutch students are taught the rule "{{lang|nl|'t&nbsp;kofschip is met thee beladen}}" ("the merchant ship is loaded with tea"). If the verb stem in the '''infinitive''' ends with one of the consonants of "{{lang|nl|'t&nbsp;kofschip}}" ({{vr|{{wrap|-t, -k, -f, -s, -ch, -p}}}}), the past tense dental is a -{{vr|t}}-; otherwise, it is a -{{vr|d}}-. However, the rule also applies to loanwords ending in -{{vr|c}}, -{{vr|q}} or -{{vr|x}}, as these are also voiceless.

