==={{vr|v}} and {{vr|z}}===

{{vr|v}} and {{vr|z}} are somewhat special:
* They are permitted only at the start of a syllable in native words, not at the end.
* For historic reasons, in native words they are never preceded by a short/lax vowel and so never occur doubled.
* When the sounds {{IPA|/v/}} and {{IPA|/z/}} occur at the end of a syllable, they are written {{vr|f}} and {{vr|s}} respectively.

Then, therefore, final devoicing is reflected in the spelling:

* {{lang|nl|blijven}} {{IPA|/ˈblɛivə(n)/}} ("to stay") → {{lang|nl|blijf}} {{IPA|/blɛif/}} "(I) stay"
* {{lang|nl|huizen}} {{IPA|/ˈɦœyzə(n)/}} "houses" → {{lang|nl|huis}} {{IPA|/ɦœys/}} "house"

However, {{vr|f}} and {{vr|s}} are also written at the end of a syllable that is not final. The pronunciation remains voiced even if the spelling shows a voiceless consonant. This is most common in the past tense forms of weak verbs:

* {{lang|nl|leven}} {{IPA|/ˈleːvə(n)/}} ("to live") → {{lang|nl|lee'''fd'''e}} {{IPA|/ˈleːvdə/}} "(I) lived"
* {{lang|nl|blozen}} {{IPA|/ˈbloːzə(n)/}} ("to blush") → {{lang|nl|bloo'''sd'''e}} {{IPA|/ˈbloːzdə/}} "(I) blushed"

Compare this to verbs in which the final consonant is underlyingly voiceless. Here, the dental assimilation rule calls for the ending {{lang|nl|-te}} after voiceless consonants:

* {{lang|nl|blaffen}} {{IPA|/ˈblɑfə(n)/}} ("to bark") → {{lang|nl|bla'''ft'''e}} {{IPA|/ˈblɑftə/}} "(I) barked"
* {{lang|nl|ruisen}} {{IPA|/ˈrœysə(n)/}} ("to rustle, to hiss") → {{lang|nl|rui'''st'''e}} {{IPA|/ˈrœystə/}} "(I) rustled"

Some modern loanwords and new coinages do not follow these rules. However, these words tend to not follow the other spelling rules as well: {{lang|nl|buzzen}} ("to page (call on a pager)") → {{lang|nl|buzz}} ("(I) page"), {{lang|nl|buzzde}} ("(I) paged").

==Diacritics==
Dutch uses the [[acute accent]] to mark stress and the [[diaeresis (diacritic)|diaeresis]] (trema) to disambiguate diphthongs/triphthongs. Occasionally, other diacritics are used in loanwords and native onomatopoeic words. Accents are not necessarily placed on capital letters (for example, the word {{lang|nl|Eén}} at the beginning of a sentence) unless the whole word is written in capitals.<ref>[http://www.onzetaal.nl/taaladvies/advies/accenten-op-hoofdletters Onze Taal: Accenten op hoofdletters (in Dutch)]</ref>

===Acute accent===
[[File:VRT Eén logo.svg|thumb|Former logo of ''[[Eén]]'' (''One''), a Flemish TV station in Belgium]]
Acute accents may be used to emphasise a word in a phrase, on the vowel in the stressed syllable. If the vowel is written as a digraph, an acute accent is put on both parts of the digraph. Although that rule includes {{vr|[[IJ (digraph)|ij]]}}, the acute accent on the {{vr|j}} is frequently omitted in typing (resulting in {{vr|íj}} instead of {{vr|íj́}}), as putting an acute accent on a {{vr|j}} is still problematic in most word processing software.<ref name="klemtoon">{{Cite web |title=Klemtoonteken (algemeen) - Taaladvies.net |url=https://taaladvies.net/klemtoonteken-algemeen/ |access-date=2022-11-14 |website=taaladvies.net|date=12 May 2021 }}</ref> If the vowel is written as more than two letters, the accent is put on the first two vowel letters – except when the first letter is a capital one. According to the [[Taalunie]], accents on capital letters are used only in [[all caps]] and in loanwords.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Accenttekens en andere diakritische tekens op hoofdletters - Taaladvies.net |url=https://taaladvies.net/accenttekens-en-andere-diakritische-tekens-op-hoofdletters/ |access-date=2022-11-14 |website=taaladvies.net|date=12 May 2021 }}</ref> So, it is correct to write {{lang|nl|één, Eén}}, and {{lang|nl|ÉÉN}}, but not to write *{{lang|nl|Één}}. The [[Genootschap Onze Taal]] states that accents can be put on capital letters whenever the need arises, but makes an exception for {{lang|nl|Eén}}.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Accenten op hoofdletters |url=https://onzetaal.nl/taalloket/accenten-op-hoofdletters |access-date=2022-11-14 |website=Onze Taal |language=nl}}</ref>

Stress on a short vowel, written with only one letter, is occasionally marked with a [[grave accent]]: {{lang|nl|Kàn jij dat?}} (equivalent to the example below), {{lang|nl|[[wikt:wel|wèl]]}}<!--translation of "wel" strongly depends on context-->. However, it is technically incorrect to do so.<ref name="klemtoon" />

Additionally, the acute accent may also be used to mark different meanings of various words, including {{lang|nl|een/één}} ([[indefinite article|a(n)]]/[[1 (number)|one]]), {{lang|nl|voor/vóór}} (for/before), {{lang|nl|vóórkomen/voorkómen}} (to occur/to prevent), and {{lang|nl|vérstrekkend/verstrékkend}} (far-reaching/issuing), as shown in the examples below.

