===Diaeresis===
A [[Diaeresis (diacritic)|diaeresis]] is used to mark a [[Hiatus (linguistics)|hiatus]], if the combination of vowel letters may be either mistaken for a digraph or interpreted in more than one way: {{lang|nl|geïnd}} (collected), {{lang|nl|geüpload}} (uploaded), {{lang|nl|egoïstisch}} (egoistic), {{lang|nl|sympathieën}} (sympathies, preferences), {{lang|nl|coördinaat}} (coordinate), {{lang|nl|reëel}} (realistic), {{lang|nl|zeeën}} (seas), {{lang|nl|met z'n tweeën}} (two together; the two of them) and even until 1996 {{lang|nl|zeeëend}} (sea duck; now spelled {{lang|nl|zee-eend}}). On a line break that separates the vowels but keeps parts of a digraph together, the diaeresis becomes redundant and so is not written: ego-/istisch, sympathie-/en, re-/eel, zee-/en, met z'n twee-/en. The rule can be extended to names, such as {{lang|nl|Michaëlla}}, e.g. [[Michaëlla Krajicek]]. The diaeresis is only used in derivational suffixes since 1996; compounds are written with a hyphen, for example {{lang|nl|auto-ongeluk}} (car accident).

===Other diacritics===
The [[grave accent]] is used in some French loanwords and native [[onomatopoeia|onomatopoeic]] words, generally when pronunciation would be wrong without it, such as {{lang|nl|[[après-ski]]}}, {{lang|nl|barrière}} (barrier), {{lang|nl|bèta, caissière}} (female cashier), {{lang|nl|carrière}} (career) and {{lang|nl|hè?}} ("What?"), {{lang|nl|blèren}} (to yell). Officially, {{lang|nl|appel}} is always written without an accent, but sometimes an accent is used to distinguish between {{lang|nl|appel}} ("apple") and {{lang|nl|appèl}} ("appeal", "roll call", and others).<ref>[http://www.onzetaal.nl/taaladvies/advies/appel-appl Onze Taal: appel / appèl] (in Dutch)</ref>

Besides being used to mark stress, [[acute accent]]s are also used in many loanwords (mainly from French) such as {{lang|nl|logé}} (overnight guest), {{lang|nl|coupé}} (train compartment), {{lang|nl|oké}} (okay) and {{lang|nl|café}}. The name of the Dutch town [[Enschede]], pronounced {{IPA|[ˈɛnsxəˌde]}} was once upon a time written Enschedé, but later the acute accent fell off without changing the pronunciation, which has '''not''' become {{IPA|*[ɛnˈsxedə]}}.

Similarly, a [[circumflex accent]] is also used in some French loanwords, including {{lang|nl|enquête}} (survey), and {{lang|nl|fêteren}} (to treat). For {{lang|nl|gênant}} (embarrassing) it is indecisive, the official spelling has the accent, but the Genootschap Onze also allows the spelling without the accent since it makes no difference to the pronunciation.<ref>[https://onzetaal.nl/taaladvies/accent-circonflexe/ Onze Taal: genant / gênant] (in Dutch)</ref> The circumflex accent is also used the [[West Frisian language]] and so in general Dutch as well if there is no translation. {{lang|nl|Skûtsjesilen}} is the most common example, where {{lang|nl|silen}} is West Frisian for {{lang|nl|zeilen}} (to sail) and a {{lang|nl|skûtsje}} is a specific type of sailboat. {{lang|nl|Fryslân}}, the official (and Frisian) name of the province [[Friesland]], is also well known, at least in the Netherlands.

==Apostrophe==
{{Unreferenced section|date=March 2020}}As in English, an apostrophe is used to mark omission of a part of word or several words:
{|
|{{lang|nl|'n}}||({{lang|nl| een}})||a, an
|-
|{{lang|nl|'t}}||({{lang|nl|het}})||it/the
|-
|{{lang|nl|'k}}||({{lang|nl|ik}})||I
|-
|{{lang|nl|'r}}||({{lang|nl|haar}})||her
|-
|{{lang|nl|m'n}}||({{lang|nl|mijn}})||my
|-
|{{lang|nl|z'n}}||({{lang|nl|zijn}})||his
|-
|{{lang|nl|zo'n}}||({{lang|nl|zo een}})||such a(n)
|-
|{{lang|nl|'s ochtends}}||({{lang|nl|des ochtends}} (archaic))||in the morning
|-
|{{lang|nl|'s middags}}||({{lang|nl|des middags}} (archaic))||in the afternoon
|-
|{{lang|nl|'s avonds}}||({{lang|nl|des avonds}} (archaic))||in the evening
|-
|{{lang|nl|'s nachts}}||({{lang|nl|des nachts}} (archaic))||in the night
|-
|{{lang|nl|'s zomers}}||({{lang|nl|des zomers}} (archaic))||in the summer
|-
|{{lang|nl|'s winters}}||({{lang|nl|des winters}} (archaic))||in the winter
|-
|{{lang|nl|'s-Gravenhage}} (formal)||({{lang|nl|des Graven hage}} (archaic))||[[The Hague|Den Haag]] (The Hague)
|-
|{{lang|nl|'s-Hertogenbosch}}||({{lang|nl|des Hertogen bosch}} (archaic))||[[’s-Hertogenbosch]]
|-
|{{lang|nl|A'dam}} (informal)||({{lang|nl|Amsterdam}})||[[Amsterdam]]
|-
|{{lang|nl|R'dam}} (informal)||({{lang|nl|Rotterdam}})||[[Rotterdam]]
|}
Contrary to the city of Den Haag, 's-Hertogenbosch (also known colloquially as ({{lang|nl|Den Bosch}}) has decided to retain the more formal orthography of its name for common communication like road signing.

Except in [[all caps]], the letter immediately following a word-initial apostrophe is not capitalised. If necessary, the second word is capitalised instead:
:{{lang|nl|'s Avonds is zij nooit thuis.}} (In the evening, she is never at home.).

